aised the quality score from 71.2 out of 100 to 98.7 out of 100 by removing 14 problematic documents, reducing the dataset from 148 to 134 documents. The cleaned dataset contains 53 billing documents, 47 shipping documents, and 34 technical documents, yielding an imbalance ratio of 1.56. PII was successfully reduced from 18 documents to 0 documents, and 1 document still contains garbled characters.\n\n**Data quality:** 71.2/100 before cleaning, 98.7/100 after \u00b7 **Documents:** 148 \u2192 134\n\n### Findings\n- **[INFO] Overall data quality score improved after cleaning.** Data cleaning raised the quality score from 71.2 out of 100 to 98.7 out of 100 by removing 14 problematic documents, reducing the dataset from 148 to 134 documents. *Use the cleaned dataset of 134 documents for baseline model training.* `cleaning_summary_001, txt_quality_score_001, txt_quality_score_002`\n- **[WARNING] Potential label misclassifications detected.** Centroid similarity analysis showed 97.12% agreement with assigned categories, identifying 4 documents that read more like another label. *Manually review and re-label the 4 flagged documents prior to model training.* `txt_labels_001`\n- **[INFO] PII present in raw text fully masked.** Raw data contained 18 documents with personal data representing 12.16% of all documents, which was successfully reduced to 0 documents and 0.0% share in the cleaned dataset. *Ensure PII masking scripts are integrated into production data ingestion pipelines.* `txt_pii_001, txt_pii_002`\n- **[WARNING] Duplicates and cross-label ambiguities present in raw text.** Raw corpus analysis revealed 4 exact extra copies, 5 near-duplicate extra copies, and 1 group appearing across multiple labels. *Remove duplicate tickets and eliminate cross-label duplicates to prevent test set contamination.* `txt_dupes_001`\n- **[WARNING] Widespread email confidentiality boilerplate detected.** A single recurring confidentiality disclaimer appeared in 57 documents, representing 38.51% of the entire raw document set. *Strip standardized disclaimers and footers so feature extraction focuses on ticket content.* `txt_boilerplate_001`\n- **[INFO] Non-English support tickets filtered out.** The raw dataset contained 5 documents in non-English languages, whereas after language filtering 0 non-English documents remain and English accounts for 100.0% of documents. *Route non-English tickets to language-specific classifiers or translation preprocessing steps.* `txt_language_001, txt_language_002`\n- **[INFO] Moderate label balance maintained across ticket classes.** The cleaned dataset contains 53 billing documents, 47 shipping documents, and 34 technical documents, yielding an imbalance ratio of 1.56. *Use stratified split strategies during train-test partitioning to maintain balanced class proportions.* `txt_balance_002`\n- **[WARNING] Encoding damage and garbled characters remain.** Raw text analysis identified 3 documents with encoding damage, and after cleaning, 1 document still contains garbled characters. *Manually inspect and repair the remaining document with encoding damage to prevent tokenization issues.* `txt_quality_001, txt_quality_002`\n\n### Next steps\n1. Inspect the 1 document containing garbled characters due to remaining encoding damage.\n1. Review the 4 documents flagged by centroid similarity as potential label misclassifications.\n1. Proceed with training the support ticket classifier on the 134 cleaned documents.", "tiles": "<div class=\"tiles\"><div class=\"tile\"><div class=\"k\">Data quality before</div><div class=\"v\">71.2/100</div></div><div class=\"tile\"><div class=\"k\">After cleaning</div><div class=\"v\">98.7/100</div></div><div class=\"tile\"><div class=\"k\">Documents</div><div class=\"v\">148 \u2192 134</div></div><div class=\"tile\"><div class=\"k\">Findings</div><div class=\"v\">8</div></div></div>", "charts": [{"data": [{"marker": {"color": "#B5471B"}, "x": ["billing", "shipping", "technical"], "y": [57, 52